WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.929
Hash usage changes over time.

2
00:00:01.929 --> 00:00:07.716
Algorithms become deprecated, compliance requirements change, libraries are replaced, and stored digest formats need migration.

3
00:00:07.716 --> 00:00:13.116
A secure design should assume that today's approved choice may need to evolve.

4
00:00:13.116 --> 00:00:20.060
Versioned formats help systems recognize which algorithm and parameters were used without guessing from length or surrounding context.

5
00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:25.847
They also make failure cases clearer when a service encounters data it no longer supports.

6
00:00:25.847 --> 00:00:29.705
Compatibility windows should be intentional and time-bounded where possible.

7
00:00:29.705 --> 00:00:38.964
A system may need to read an old digest format while writing a new one, but that should not silently preserve weak policy forever.

8
00:00:38.964 --> 00:00:46.293
Migration behavior should be tested with old values, new values, invalid encodings, unsupported algorithms, wrong lengths, and failed upgrades.

9
00:00:46.293 --> 00:00:51.694
Operational teams should know how to measure migration progress and remove legacy support safely.

10
00:00:51.694 --> 00:00:56.709
Dashboards or reports can track remaining legacy records without exposing sensitive input data.

11
00:00:56.709 --> 00:01:03.653
Code review should flag deprecated algorithms, custom hash wrappers, unclear encoding, weak or unexplained digest lengths, unsafe truncation,

12
00:01:03.653 --> 00:01:08.668
password misuse, missing salts, and plain hashes used where authenticated integrity is required.

13
00:01:08.668 --> 00:01:13.298
Dependency review should confirm that cryptographic libraries are maintained and configured correctly.

14
00:01:13.298 --> 00:01:18.699
Governance keeps the same mistakes from returning under new helper names or compatibility flags.

15
00:01:18.699 --> 00:01:26.800
Architecture notes should record the approved patterns so new services do not need to rediscover the rules during a rushed integration.
